WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.379 --> 00:00:01.462
(audience clapping)
- Gorgeous.

00:00:01.462 --> 00:00:03.361
- Thank you so much.

00:00:03.361 --> 00:00:04.430
- Oh my gosh!

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.360
- My friend Carly Mark, designed this.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.940
She has a company called
"Puppets and Puppets".

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.380
It's very sculptural.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.430
- It's very cool.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:12.390
You walked out and I was like,

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.160
you were like a walking piece of art.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.750
- Oh, that's the nicest
thing I've ever heard.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.510
Well, I was watching you from
backstage thinking "What--

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.253
- All the horses
- A razzle, dazzle queen.

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:21.425
- I wore all the horses (laughs).

00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:23.820
- I love every one of those horses.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.380
- So people, normally, I mean,

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.520
you do look like a fashionista.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.650
So people usually associate
you with New York,

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.030
but you're now in London, right?

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.020
- I am in London.
- Yeah.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.340
- I married an English man,

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:33.277
and I stayed.
- Yeah.

00:00:33.277 --> 00:00:34.650
Do you love it? I love London.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.150
- I love it so much.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:36.983
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:36.983 --> 00:00:39.750
- I find it, the architecture's beautiful.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.360
The culture is, you know...

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.860
There's incredible history.
- [Kelly] History.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.600
Yeah.
- There's incredible art.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.880
The challenge I find is snacks.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.204
- Ah, this is what I find
challenging on tour (laughs).

00:00:51.204 --> 00:00:52.221
Yeah. See.
- That's--

00:00:52.221 --> 00:00:53.054
- Nothing anymore--

00:00:53.054 --> 00:00:53.970
- It's just different.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.980
- It's different, firstly,
the range of snacks

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:56.813
is different.
- Yeah.

00:00:56.813 --> 00:00:58.350
- If you're a very snack focused person,

00:00:58.350 --> 00:00:59.430
it's hard.
- Yep, yep.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:03.450
And secondly, everything
closes at like nine o'clock.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.576
I'm from New York where--

00:01:04.576 --> 00:01:05.409
- [Kelly] 24/7.

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:06.242
- 24/7.

00:01:06.242 --> 00:01:07.740
This is an example, like I was depressed

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.680
at four in the morning in New
York, a couple of years ago,

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.980
got on Postmates and I
was able to order a burger

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.855
on two patties of ramen.

00:01:15.855 --> 00:01:18.870
(Kelly and audience laugh)
At four in the morning.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:19.800
- It's a beautiful city (laughing).

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.153
- It's a beautiful city.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.310
I don't care about the,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.700
I don't care about, you know,
the, like big buildings.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.288
I don't care about,
you know, Central Park.

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:30.121
- [Kelly] It's the Ramen burger

00:01:30.121 --> 00:01:31.095
- I care about the Ramen burger.

00:01:31.095 --> 00:01:32.370
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.110
- So wait, so have you had
time to make your place?

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.060
Are you into like design and stuff?

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.194
You know, interior design
with your new place?

00:01:37.194 --> 00:01:38.190
- I love it.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:39.023
It's my passion.

00:01:39.023 --> 00:01:40.380
I like to keep the...

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.220
Do you love interior design?

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.150
- I love it!

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.140
- It's like, to me it's--

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:43.973
- Nesting.

00:01:43.973 --> 00:01:44.806
- Nesting.

00:01:44.806 --> 00:01:47.010
It's the most relaxing thing
to be able to make your space.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.140
I mean, just your dressing
rooms here are iconic.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.837
I walked in and I went,
I could live in here.

00:01:50.837 --> 00:01:52.110
- That's my art department.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:52.943
They're so great.

00:01:52.943 --> 00:01:53.790
- Well, they're amazing.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.470
But I try to be my own art department.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:59.010
So I have, in the, I've
painted every wall.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.470
I've muraled, I've, you know, made...

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.240
You guys are gonna get a little sense.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.950
Gotten lots of stuff from vintage store.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:05.820
- [Kelly] You're my sister.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:06.990
She's creative like that.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:08.683
She does it herself as well.

00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:11.250
- [Lena] Those are my furry stools.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.083
- [Kelly] Oh my Gosh!

00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:13.530
- There's a woman named
Uzumaki who makes those

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.210
and people walk into the
kitchen and they scream

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.940
because they think I have
like three, sort of...

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:18.773
- Random animals?

00:02:18.773 --> 00:02:21.450
- Random animals whose
hairs have been dyed

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.580
with Manic Panic, but
no, those are my stools.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:24.413
- Do you have animals?

00:02:24.413 --> 00:02:25.830
'cause, do they attack that?

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:26.663
- We do have,

00:02:26.663 --> 00:02:27.840
We have two cats and two dogs.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.103
And the funny thing is
our dogs are hairless.

00:02:30.103 --> 00:02:31.110
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.993
So our stools have hair,
but our dogs don't.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.560
- I love you and your
husband, just so much already.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.460
- Thank you so much.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:39.630
And I love doing it.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.557
And for a while, my husband was just like

00:02:41.557 --> 00:02:43.620
"Yes, you do you, it's..."

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.120
But then I think I've empowered him

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.620
a little bit too much, and now--

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.490
- Oh well he's creative.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:48.570
He's in the music world. Yeah.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:49.403
- He is.

00:02:49.403 --> 00:02:50.236
He's, there he is.

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:51.069
I miss him.

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:53.040
- So now he likes to be a part of it too.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:53.873
- Now he likes--

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:54.706
- So are there battles of like, "Oh!"

00:02:54.706 --> 00:02:55.680
- Well, Yes.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.210
Like now he's having opinions

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.841
and we can't have men having those.

00:02:58.841 --> 00:03:00.810
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:02.370
- He's like, So I live here too.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:05.010
- Yeah. And I was like, that's
not what I signed up for.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.150
I signed up for complete control.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.170
- You are changing.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.330
- That's exactly right.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:10.650
- So you guys just celebrated this year,

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:11.670
your one year anniversary?

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:12.503
- We did.

00:03:12.503 --> 00:03:13.336
- Congratulations.

00:03:13.336 --> 00:03:14.190
(audience applauding)
- Thank you.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:15.023
- That's so cool.

00:03:15.023 --> 00:03:18.210
- I didn't think I, I did not
ever think I'd get married.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.800
I didn't think I was
gonna be a marriage person

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.420
but I'm enjoying it tremendously.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.340
- Yeah. That's awesome.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.600
I never thought I'd get married.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:25.433
It didn't work for me.

00:03:25.433 --> 00:03:27.720
But I, but I never, I never
thought I'd get married though.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:28.553
I didn't.

00:03:28.553 --> 00:03:29.386
And then it can be wonderful.

00:03:29.386 --> 00:03:31.470
- Well what I love about you and this show

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.270
is how open you are
because so many people,

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.500
we know we live in a world

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.660
where people wanna be glossy and perfect.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:37.493
And the fact--

00:03:37.493 --> 00:03:38.326
- I am unfiltered.

00:03:38.326 --> 00:03:39.159
- The fact that you come out here

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:40.365
and you're just like, yeah, marriage.

00:03:40.365 --> 00:03:41.613
(blows raspberry) Not, not it, like.

00:03:41.613 --> 00:03:43.127
- It's not for everyone. Yeah.

00:03:43.127 --> 00:03:46.140
- No. And I, I think I had to obviously,

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.370
find someone very specific

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.870
who would be down to do marriage

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.460
the way that I wanna do marriage.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:51.293
- Absolutely.

00:03:51.293 --> 00:03:52.890
Yeah. I'm not against it. I
think it's a beautiful thing.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.263
And I think it's so wonderful too.

00:03:54.263 --> 00:03:56.173
Even like, you know, we have a,

00:03:56.173 --> 00:03:58.260
a couple on today, later in the show,

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.780
and they're like older
and they just got married,

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.670
and they're like 80s and
90s and I'm just like,

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:03.503
that's beautiful.

00:04:03.503 --> 00:04:04.418
I think it's beautiful.

00:04:04.418 --> 00:04:05.251
- I think it's beau--

00:04:05.251 --> 00:04:07.290
- But I do think we all,
because of how, you know,

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.290
we grew up, we all, our love
languages are different.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.123
You have to find someone

00:04:11.123 --> 00:04:12.360
that kind of matches you,

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.470
and you grow together,

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:14.303
I think that's important.

00:04:14.303 --> 00:04:15.330
- And think it's beautiful
if you're getting to do it,

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.740
Like if you're doing
it in your 80s and 90s,

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.120
you're doing it on your own terms.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.940
And I think so many of us were sold

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.890
a specific bill of goods about what,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:24.270
sort of a heterosexual partnership

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.500
was supposed to look like.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.333
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:26.333 --> 00:04:28.770
- And I think one of the
benefits of, you know,

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.805
having had a little
experience under my belt

00:04:30.805 --> 00:04:32.584
before I got married was I was like.

00:04:32.584 --> 00:04:33.417
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:33.417 --> 00:04:35.924
- I was up for, living a
very different kind of life

00:04:35.924 --> 00:04:37.710
where I was doing it on my own.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.440
And then he just happened
to make that life better.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.280
- Oh that's so cool.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.203
You found something that
compliments you. That's amazing.

00:04:43.203 --> 00:04:44.850
- And I hope I compliment him.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.040
I keep talking about how he
has to follow all my rules

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.120
and how he compliments me.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.110
- We know you're kidding. We know,

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:49.943
yeah.
- He's, okay thank you.

00:04:49.943 --> 00:04:50.776
- We know you're being funny.

00:04:50.776 --> 00:04:51.609
- Thank you.

00:04:51.609 --> 00:04:53.275
(audience clapping)
- So you have a podcast

00:04:53.275 --> 00:04:54.108
called The C-Word.

00:04:54.108 --> 00:04:57.210
Now, a lot of C words out
there, like which C word?

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.110
- It's a great question,

00:04:58.110 --> 00:04:59.400
'cause there's so many fun C words

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.660
to choose from.
- There really are. Yeah.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.580
- And some of them I really, in England,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.590
there's a few that are really popular

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:05.745
that aren't as popular in the US.

00:05:05.745 --> 00:05:07.140
- I know of one right now.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.010
- You know of one right now?

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:08.843
- And they say it and I'm like

00:05:08.843 --> 00:05:10.093
"Oh we're just freely saying that one."

00:05:10.093 --> 00:05:11.850
- I know. We're just letting that one go.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.117
And I know and I'm always kind of like

00:05:13.117 --> 00:05:14.347
"Are we really allowed to do this?

00:05:14.347 --> 00:05:15.420
"Okay, I'll jump right in."

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:16.253
- Yeah.

00:05:16.253 --> 00:05:18.600
- But our word, our C word is crazy.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.480
And it's about the way that women in,

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.840
often in the public eye
are perceived as crazy.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.400
Whether it's by the press

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.880
or by fandoms or by culture at large.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.920
And really trying to examine

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.320
why we ascribe certain labels to women.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.640
I feel like, culturally right now,

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.208
we're having a moment
where we're revisiting

00:05:37.208 --> 00:05:39.090
certain ways we've treated women.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.410
Whether it's looking at the way

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.240
that we publicly dealt with Britney Spears

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.464
or certain starlets of the 2000s,

00:05:44.464 --> 00:05:46.897
going "Actually the way that we spoke to

00:05:46.897 --> 00:05:49.320
"and about this woman wasn't okay."

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.650
We've done an episode on Amy
Winehouse, Whitney Houston.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:56.460
Iconic, important women,
who have also been labeled,

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.300
particularly by this
culture we live in, America,

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.010
as crazy or other.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.050
And my partner who I do
it with, Alissa Bennett,

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.330
who's also my best friend, I'm very lucky.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:09.540
She is, a historian and a cultural critic.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.750
So she really gets in, and
digs in, and does the research.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:13.583
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:13.583 --> 00:06:14.910
- And then I try to take my experience

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:17.400
being publicly called crazy
and then we have a soup.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.247
- Yeah, no I love that.
(audience clapping)

00:06:19.247 --> 00:06:21.840
You know, it's interesting
even bringing up like, Whitney,

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.711
'cause that whole documentary
came out about her

00:06:23.711 --> 00:06:26.760
and like a lot of us fans
didn't know all that background.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.980
So that's the thing I think
we need to keep in our minds.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:30.360
Like you don't know what people

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:31.680
are going through or have been through.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.810
And then to label them that,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.970
is so detrimental to them emotionally,

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.790
because you have no idea,
what has been put on them,

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.050
like from, since childhood, you know.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.600
- That's so much what
the podcast is about,

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.010
is about looking at the fact

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:46.890
that these people have lived
big, full, complicated lives

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:50.010
and often been encouraged
to keep a lot of that secret

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:51.537
because of the way that we want our--

00:06:51.537 --> 00:06:52.370
- 'cause they're a product.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:53.430
- 'cause they're a product.
- To someone.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:54.263
Yeah.
- That's,

00:06:54.263 --> 00:06:55.096
and you would know about,

00:06:55.096 --> 00:06:56.160
I mean you started in this business young.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.240
- Never. I've never been there,

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:58.073
myself.
- You've never known

00:06:58.073 --> 00:06:58.906
what that feels like.
- No.

00:06:58.906 --> 00:06:59.970
Never had anyone treat me like a product.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.310
- No. So that's, that's the thing is also

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:03.570
being treated like a product

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.360
makes you wanna push back and push out

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.827
and then kind of,

00:07:08.827 --> 00:07:09.840
"misbehave".

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.460
But where does that really come from?

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:12.293
- Yeah.

00:07:12.293 --> 00:07:13.126
- We just started our season

00:07:13.126 --> 00:07:14.340
with a two-parter on Lindsay Lohan,

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.530
can't wait for this
Netflix Christmas movie.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.660
I am gonna be the first person

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.520
who watches Lindsay
Lohan's Christmas movie.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:21.353
I'm just gonna be--

00:07:21.353 --> 00:07:22.963
- I love Christmas movies. First of all.

00:07:22.963 --> 00:07:25.380
- I love Christmas movies.
I love Lindsay Lohan.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.020
I'm gonna be stuffing my face
with popcorn and cheering.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:28.980
- I think that's the thing too,

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.080
I tell artists on The
Voice that I work with

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.730
'cause there's a lot of young
ones that I work with usually

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:36.480
and I'm like, quit, like do
not aim to please everyone.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:37.380
Like it's never going to work.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.240
Like do you, There's gonna
be an audience for it

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.740
and there's gonna be
a whole mess of people

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:44.160
that are like not vibing
with you. And that's okay.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:45.450
Like, but I think people think

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.940
they have to, they wanna
be all to all people

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.200
and it's, that's exhausting.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.033
No one can do that.

00:07:50.033 --> 00:07:50.866
- No.
- Yeah.

00:07:50.866 --> 00:07:52.350
- There's an amazing Nina Simone quote,

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:53.636
and I'm gonna butcher it slightly.

00:07:53.636 --> 00:07:54.570
- Can't wait.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:56.126
- But, you're excited for me to just but--

00:07:56.126 --> 00:07:56.959
- Yeah.

00:07:56.959 --> 00:07:57.792
- But it's a beautiful quote, which is

00:07:57.792 --> 00:08:01.320
you are, you lose everything you've got

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.150
trying to give everyone what they want.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:03.983
And--

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:04.816
- Yeah.

00:08:04.816 --> 00:08:07.080
- When I heard that and then butchered it,

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.200
it really spoke to me in
a deep and profound way.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.250
- Yeah.
- And I think,

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:15.360
those people are so
lucky that they have you,

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.160
to be that guide for them.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.290
They're so lucky that they have you,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:20.310
someone with experience.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.170
Because if I'd had sort
of an older person,

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:23.787
older woman with experience telling me,

00:08:23.787 --> 00:08:25.207
You know what?
- I wish I would've had that.

00:08:25.207 --> 00:08:26.820
- "This is what it's gonna look like."

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.290
I think my life would've,

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:29.880
I mean I'm very happy with where I am,

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:30.713
but I also think that--

00:08:30.713 --> 00:08:32.880
- My 20s would've been a lot smoother.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.050
- I would've spent a lot less time

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:35.490
crying into that Ramen burger.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:36.390
- Yeah. (audience laughing)

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.551
I just, crying in
general. Yeah. I was like.

00:08:38.551 --> 00:08:41.250
So you're also highlighting strong women

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:42.840
in your new movie
"Catherine Called Birdy".

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.257
So what is "Catherine Called Birdy"?

00:08:44.257 --> 00:08:45.240
- "Catherine Called Birdy" is a movie

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:46.619
that's based on my favorite
book from childhood

00:08:46.619 --> 00:08:49.260
which was called, shocker,
"Catherine Called Birdy".

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.580
(Kelly laughing)
And it's about--

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:51.681
- Where did you come up with the title

00:08:51.681 --> 00:08:53.220
for the movie?
- I know!

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:54.861
And it's about a young woman,

00:08:54.861 --> 00:08:58.410
a 14 year old in 1290 Medieval England

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.470
who is living her life,
loving her childhood,

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:02.520
getting to kind of have that like,

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.950
rough and tumble experience
of being a little kid,

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:07.350
and then gets her period and
her parents basically decide

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:08.460
it's time to marry her off.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:12.210
And it's about her fight to
maintain her independence.

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:13.740
But it is a comedy.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:14.610
- Yeah, I love that.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:15.443
- And so--

00:09:15.443 --> 00:09:16.620
- I would've just never told anyone

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:17.640
I started my period. (chuckles)

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:18.481
- Well she tries that,

00:09:18.481 --> 00:09:19.314
- Yeah.

00:09:19.314 --> 00:09:20.147
- And it doesn't work.

00:09:20.147 --> 00:09:21.120
And so it's that story,

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.880
the story of her kind of trying
to maintain her independence

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:24.990
and her sense of self,

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.210
and really coming up against the idea

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.220
of what women can and can't do.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:30.053
And I think what's funny

00:09:30.053 --> 00:09:31.380
is it takes place a thousand years ago,

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.760
but, almost a thousand years ago.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.310
But there's a lot in
there that still feels.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:36.143
- Relevant

00:09:36.143 --> 00:09:38.910
- Very relevant because we
still live in a culture,

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:40.380
as we discussed, that puts a lot of very,

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:42.960
specific constraints and demands on,

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:45.110
not just its young
women, its young people.

